/b458e7ce396e2645405ae9ea0b183f63221f0e5b935ce41459202d3714e3b25a" :answer-field/choices ["q-352424/field-2/b458e7ce396e2645405ae9ea0b183f63221f0e5b935ce41459202d3714e3b25a" "q-352424/field-2/aa8b3f9c78bb7b825079ad4aaf338d07c96e446b686a39a5121216be4228efdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352424/field-3/a8a725fec0b63a3b8d6f4591903bfe81596da23bd6deceff5c00414df35bbd3f" :answer/id #uuid "86a9cf1a-8457-568c-a7eb-1ee851678de3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "independent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352424/field-3/988842f9ae7a51d1679b23c13893e9f8d3070ea423527b72c2d8836642aabb0a" :answer/id #uuid "f7f9a3de-049a-521d-bc03-ec3d92587a07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "dependent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352424/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f04d1102-b0e0-53e9-80d3-27b9ebcaf664" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-352424/field-3/988842f9ae7a51d1679b23c13893e9f8d3070ea423527b72c2d8836642aabb0a" :answer-field/choices ["q-352424/field-3/a8a725fec0b63a3b8d6f4591903bfe81596da23bd6deceff5c00414df35bbd3f" "q-352424/field-3/988842f9ae7a51d1679b23c13893e9f8d3070ea423527b72c2d8836642aabb0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352424" :question/id #uuid "3aa6e04c-c502-538f-a2a5-b3ef476e8423" :question/math-academy-id "q-352424" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -10{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5{e}^{3t})\\cdot (2{e}^{3t})-(-{e}^{3t})\\cdot (-10{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{6t}-10{e}^{6t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352424/field-1" "q-352424/field-2" "q-352424/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352424"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333891/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "4ed62654-3135-59fd-b0d5-51e3b5779c2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333891/field-1/01a112cc320e7cabee371bd784cf7258e67f9024691d652f5ab0abfa895fe0fe" :answer/id #uuid "eda98113-baf9-5a08-b589-df97d0a1cc32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+25{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333891/field-1/c666c90274f6b091243279ec720573c89f9f52811f1335ebaa08b6634427f9c