(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\cdot (-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))-(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333891/field-1" "q-333891/field-2" "q-333891/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333891"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333741/field-1/53e4a737611060a528e7b7d8d5724b5d2c38eccae55b91ac9a53e3b365e0e732" :answer/id #uuid "7f622c03-7a9f-56c5-87a0-80711209868e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333741/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "0fd0df03-7647-57db-8927-178cbe0533a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answ