_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))-(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333895/field-1" "q-333895/field-2" "q-333895/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220561 :answer-field/correct 17592186220562 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220569 :answer-field/correct 17592186220571 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220566 :answer-field/correct 17592186220568 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333862"] :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220061 :answer-field/correct 17592186220063 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220064 :answer-field/correct 17592186220066 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220067 :answer-field/correct 17592186220069 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-322737/field-4/8b747009fb20d88e15399236e6cd306224cf20acd7181