type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfdd07f-9e79-5842-b92e-ef2c81f2d3b4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334527/field-4/fc66879569e6d4dd98d4596cef8019fa6a94ed867ffa0b0b1cb1786909fc6b0e" :answer-field/choices ["q-334527/field-4/4aca377c985aed02a5404e099c4ccbcc99536ccb9dc67003caba66c3a182d33e" "q-334527/field-4/fc66879569e6d4dd98d4596cef8019fa6a94ed867ffa0b0b1cb1786909fc6b0e" "q-334527/field-4/8b747009fb20d88e15399236e6cd306224cf20acd7181f47b510335bee8fef25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-5/a5293ac7e38dfeb2fca32f5db893dfe7d07d9fede1ba2b4be5e824acba2ca866" :answer/id #uuid "05e27f29-a644-58fe-b3de-0f64c484f1cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a,b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-5/b49703179306e83d85bbe4c6ac62481c17882ef850059eeb30e83821a2dfad88" :answer/id #uuid "c2badceb-c454-546f-975b-b1eff64daee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a,c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-5/1c2bd59983d2c1fb302dc9e16af330828afdf644688012d6ce01f41ba68c277a" :answer/id #uuid "20aef9a0-b55e-5b8b-838f-52ab76f1f4ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "b,c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "923c044f-c95e-538e-ba62-61a2edf5f309" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334527/field-5/a5293ac7e38dfeb2fca32f5db893dfe7d07d9fede1ba2b4be5e824acba2ca866" :answer-field/choices ["q-334527/field-5/a5293ac7e38dfeb2fca32f5db893dfe7d07d9fede1ba2b4be5e824acba2ca866" "q-334527/field-5/b49703179306e83d85bbe4c6ac62481c17882ef850059eeb30e83821a2dfad88" "q-334527/field-5/1c2bd59983d2c1fb302dc9e16af330828afdf644688012d6ce01f41ba68c277a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-6/091c8773aa8eb17bd18bf788816491cc12f63fa30d47305364056b3a8a9c53bf" :answer/id #uuid "b57fb01e-94c9-5512-b5d7-3d95494d45a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-6/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a4633777-8d9e-57d9-8b6d-8fde713a46e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-6/2d88b1740e62162c443d46133933ba1baf3aa975dfa310d007b8b06ae9ac0cc3" :answer/id #uuid "984d0bd1-1c69-57c3-9083-23da10157fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-6/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8d2d50e9-70b0-5532-82d8-87d996829f66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d79f31-fa0d-5506-ace4-5ab4ea687fc6" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334527/field-6/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334527/field-6/091c8773aa8eb17bd18bf788816491cc12f63fa30d47305364056b3a8a9c53bf" "q-334527/field-6/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-334527/field-6/2d88b1740e62162c443d46133933ba1baf3aa975dfa310d007b8b06ae9ac0cc3" "q-334527/field-6/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334527" :question/id #uuid "61330a4f-0538-5700-9da8-2455820c0258" :question/math-academy-id "q-334527" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334527/field-1" "q-334527/field-2" "q-334527/field-3" "q-334527/field-4" "q-334527/field-5" "q-334527/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334527"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21179"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b6c879d367709165835c2de7cec94895897be567a9e264222506d4b2a63874e0.png)\nFind the Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ of the above vector-valued functions." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ebce3767dfd869aa1ebf4bb03b9eb06a7f41dbb4b927a7699982f35d3a43a562.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/b07428ffa056ae11d8fef349d666abab80e64bfc1822006f42db42a4b21e596d.png)\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & {t}^{3}-5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot 4-({t}^{3}-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}+10 \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21180"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/235991f16a48086f1629a0519b1358db70ee0b26062a651a2a8adc305c682681.png)\nConsider the vector-valued functions $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ above. Given that $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, compute the Wronskian is $W(\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2)$ and determine if the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent on $\\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/51774bbcf79f8ab11069a5d7c955f73cd637f8407aa7a600673de0eaf9adb7ea.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4{e}^{2t})\\cdot (2{e}^{t})-{e}^{t}\\cdot ({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =-9{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21181"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in\\mathbb R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/74e10d18890ebd27b2339e13ac2bbc9e0821c1dfea752478a90877d0a0059ff6.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f08f12db6684778c1f5734c0731a370663fcfb23cd958dde49e3a2f41cd0e50a.png) is a solution of the system.\n\n- The function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/f5c546b729e994f79408b4344979446aac8aa3820c1798251bc50378f8f7159b.png) is a solution of the system.\n\nAlso, determine which pair of solutions forms a set of fundamental solutions to the system." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(\\mathbf{x}_1,\\mathbf{x}_2)$ of two vector-valued functions \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/e61be508e4ad25ed39996a7a924a732290f3c8be7e5e953e01da6ff35c5669f4.png)\nis defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/ed9d2deba98ee27ebe8a183cab45f42a92f2d4f49733fa8a8f7c9acdf6185912.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then $\\mathbf{x}_1$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/3ac4270c91e71468086149ed819c644c71d59aea898520acc8e5fbf30205985c.png)\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/7d502d9256ace32899ea72f143f3694db8f0531efebf2dbd0c20dc857bd563b5.png) $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-4t+2 \\\\ -4{t}^{2}+8t-4\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/c223cfc85f5a1f23006dd9de1775f898731a2cce2b88d1c27c0ab3f4ee238e66.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)^{′} \\\\ (-4t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2t+1 \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (2t+1)+1\\cdot (-4t) \\\\ -4\\cdot (2t+1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066416/assets/2f5d5dd7f1fe1b1a51df2930d780fc11af9a31f8cac9cc9c38f966eb42bc848d.png) $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (1-2t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot t+1\\cdot (1-2t) \\\\ -4\\cdot t+(-2)\\cdot (1-2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(b,c) & =|\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & t \\\\ -4t & 1-2t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\cdot (1-2t)-t\\cdot (-4t) \\\\  & =(2t+1)(1-2t)+4{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t \\in \\mathbb R.$ Thus, since $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf c$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c} \\big\\}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
